    "q-100100": {
      "dom_id": "step-q100100",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14210400/assets/3cc4f4447ee6fb212222b28c7e4c31cca435c542e3d2f25ae3f3331a3b98da2b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x).$ Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)+2g(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(1+xg(x))}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=6$  and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=2.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)+2g(x))=6+2(2)=10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(1+xg(x))=1+2(2)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{f(x)+2g(x)}{1+xg(x)}=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
