      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14210400/assets/b563936f37e82c3f6a422f80b448a7902a656a2749a7abced6c7bb9225ecf2bc.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -4}f(x)=2.$ So, applying the product rule, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -4}f(x) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-4)^{2}+1)\\cdot (2) \\\\  & =66\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
